1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,559
Chapter 8. The pulpit.

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:10,358
I had not been seated very long, a man of a certain venerable robustness

3
00:00:10,359 --> 00:00:14,678
entered. Immediately, as the storm pelted door flew back upon admitting

4
00:00:14,679 --> 00:00:19,718
him, a quick, regardful eyeing of him by all of the congregation, sufficiently

5
00:00:19,719 --> 00:00:24,839
attested that this fine old man was the chaplain. Yes, it was the famous Father

6
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:30,197
Mappel, so called by the whaleman, among whom he was a very great favorite. He

7
00:00:30,198 --> 00:00:34,919
had been a sailor and a harpooner in his youth, but for many years past had

8
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:39,559
dedicated his life to the ministry. At the time I now write of, Father Mappel

9
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:44,438
was in the hardy winter of a healthy old age, that sort of old age which seems

10
00:00:44,439 --> 00:00:47,237
merging into a second flowering youth.

11
00:00:47,238 --> 00:00:51,478
For among all the fissures of his wrinkles there shown certain mild gleams

12
00:00:51,479 --> 00:00:56,438
of a newly developing bloom, the spring vger peeping forth even beneath

13
00:00:56,439 --> 00:01:01,559
February's snow. No one, having previously heard his history, could for

14
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:05,799
the first time behold Father Mappel without the utmost interest, because

15
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:10,999
there were certain engrafted clerical peculiarities about him imputable to

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,799
that adventurous maritime life he had led. When he entered, I observed that he

17
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:19,718
carried no umbrella, and certainly had not come in his carriage, for his

18
00:01:19,719 --> 00:01:24,759
tarpolin hat ran down with melting sleet, and his great pilot cloth jacket

19
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:28,919
seemed almost to drag him to the floor with the weight of the water it had

20
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:34,839
absorbed. However, hat and coat and overshoes were one by one removed, and

21
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:39,959
hung up in a little space in an adjacent corner, when arrayed in a decent suit,

22
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,919
he quietly approached the pullpit.

23
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:48,438
Like most old-fashioned pullpits, it was a very lofty one, and since a regular

24
00:01:48,439 --> 00:01:52,759
stairs to such a height would, by its long angle with the floor, seriously

25
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:57,398
contract the already small area of the chapel, the architect, it seemed, had

26
00:01:57,399 --> 00:02:02,358
acted upon the hint of Father Mappel, and finish the pulpit without a stairs,

27
00:02:02,359 --> 00:02:06,759
substituting a perpendicular side ladder, like those used in mounting a

28
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:11,559
ship from a boat at sea. The wife of a whailing captain had provided the chapel

29
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:16,438
with a handsome pair of red worsted man robes for this ladder, which being

30
00:02:16,439 --> 00:02:21,318
itself nicely headed and stained with a mahogany color, the whole contrivance,

31
00:02:21,319 --> 00:02:26,438
considering what manner of chapel it was, seemed by no means in bad taste,

32
00:02:26,439 --> 00:02:30,678
halting for an instant at the foot of the ladder, and with both hands grasping

33
00:02:30,679 --> 00:02:35,478
the ornamental knobs of the manropppes, Father Mappel cast a look upwards, and

34
00:02:35,479 --> 00:02:41,318
then, with a truly sailor-like but still reverential dexterity, hand overhand,

35
00:02:41,319 --> 00:02:45,718
mounted the steps as if ascending the main top of his vessel.

36
00:02:45,719 --> 00:02:49,718
The perpendicular parts of this side ladder, as is usually the case with

37
00:02:49,719 --> 00:02:54,678
swinging ones, were of clothcovered rope. Only the rounds were of wood, so

38
00:02:54,679 --> 00:02:59,318
that at every step there was a joint. At my first glimpse of the pulpit, it had

39
00:02:59,319 --> 00:03:03,959
not escaped me that, however convenient for a ship, these joints, in the present

40
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:08,839
instance, seemed unnecessary, for I was not prepared to see Father Mappel, after

41
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:13,478
gaining the height, slowly turn round, and stooping over the pulpit,

42
00:03:13,479 --> 00:03:18,278
deliberately drag up the ladder step by step, till the hole was deposited

43
00:03:18,279 --> 00:03:24,117
within, leaving him impregnable in his little kbeck. I pondered some time

44
00:03:24,118 --> 00:03:28,999
without fully comprehending the reason for this. Father Mappel enjoyed such a

45
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,718
wide reputation for sincerity and sanctity that I could not suspect him of

46
00:03:33,719 --> 00:03:39,237
courting notoriety by any mere tricks of the stage. No, thought I, there must be

47
00:03:39,238 --> 00:03:41,799
some sober reason for this thing.

48
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:45,478
Furthermore, it must symbolize something unseen.

49
00:03:45,479 --> 00:03:50,759
Can it be then that by that act of physical isolation, he signifies his

50
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:55,157
spiritual withdrawal for the time from all outward worldly ties and

51
00:03:55,158 --> 00:04:00,919
connections? Yes, for replenished with the meat and wine of the word to the

52
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:06,999
faithful man of God. This pulpit I see is a self-containing stronghold, a lofty

53
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,598
erandstein with a perennial well of water within the walls. But the side

54
00:04:12,599 --> 00:04:16,278
ladder was not the only strange feature of the place. Borrowed from the

55
00:04:16,279 --> 00:04:21,237
chaplain's former seafarings, between the marble senotitaps on either hand of

56
00:04:21,238 --> 00:04:25,879
the pulpit, the wall which formed its back was adorned with a large painting

57
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:30,839
representing a gallant ship beating against a terrible storm off a lee coast

58
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:36,117
of black rocks and snowy breakers. But high above the flying scud and dark

59
00:04:36,118 --> 00:04:40,278
rolling clouds, there floated a little aisle of sunlight from which beamed

60
00:04:40,279 --> 00:04:45,398
forth an angel's face, and this bright face shed a distinct spot of radiance

61
00:04:45,399 --> 00:04:50,197
upon the ship's tossed deck, something like that silver plate now inserted into

62
00:04:50,198 --> 00:04:52,999
the victo's plank where Nelson fell.

63
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,358
"Ah, noble ship," the angel seemed to say, "Beat on, beat on, thou noble ship,

64
00:04:58,359 --> 00:05:03,318
and bear a hearty helm. For lo, the sun is breaking through, the clouds are

65
00:05:03,319 --> 00:05:07,398
rolling off. Serenest azure is at hand.

66
00:05:07,399 --> 00:05:11,237
Nor was the pulpit itself without a trace of the same sea taste that had

67
00:05:11,238 --> 00:05:16,038
achieved the latter in the picture. Its paneled front was in the likeness of a

68
00:05:16,039 --> 00:05:20,759
ship's bluff boughs, and the holy Bible rested on a projecting piece of

69
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:25,718
scrollwork fashioned after a ship's fiddle-headed beak. What could be more

70
00:05:25,719 --> 00:05:31,157
full of meaning? For the pulpit is ever this earth's foremost part. All the rest

71
00:05:31,158 --> 00:05:36,678
comes in its rear. The pulpit leads the world. From thence it is, the storm of

72
00:05:36,679 --> 00:05:41,318
God's quick wrath is first described, and the bow must bear the earliest

73
00:05:41,319 --> 00:05:46,999
brunt. From thence it is, the god of breezes, fair or foul, is first invoked

74
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,197
for favorable winds. Yes, the world's a ship on its passage out, and not a

75
00:05:52,198 --> 00:05:57,920
voyage complete, and the pulpit is its prow.
